  {
   "id": "contain",
   "type": "decision",
   "prompt": "It's 22:25 UTC. What do you recommend?",
   "context": "What you know: the attacker has the admin password, a web shell and an uploaded program are on the server, that program connects out to the attacker, and the public site shows the attacker's image. The CFO wants the site back tonight; marketing is fielding calls.",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "rebuild",
     "text": "Take the server offline now, keep a disk image, rebuild from a clean backup, patch Joomla and change every credential before it goes back online.",
     "feedback": "This is the safest option for integrity, because nothing the attacker touched comes back. It costs hours or days of downtime and needs a backup you trust from before 21:45. If the backup has the same weak password and the same unpatched Joomla, you rebuild the same hole. The CFO will ask why one picture costs a day of sales."
    },
    {
     "id": "cleanup",
     "text": "Keep the site up: block both attacking IP addresses at the firewall, delete the web shell and the program, reset the admin password and add a second factor.",
     "feedback": "This is fast and cheap, and it removes everything you've found. The risk is what you haven't found. An attacker with a web shell for half an hour may have left other files or accounts, and blocking two IP addresses is easy to get around. You're betting your search was complete."
    },
    {
     "id": "isolate",
     "text": "Replace the site with a static maintenance page, cut the server off from the internet, capture memory and disk, and decide between clean-up and rebuild once you know the scope.",
     "feedback": "This balances evidence against business needs: the defacement stops being public, and you keep what you need to learn what else happened and to support an insurance or legal claim. It takes the most staff time and leaves the decision open longest, which the CFO may hear as 'we don't know'."
    },
    {
     "id": "watch",
     "text": "Leave everything running for 24 hours and watch the attacker, to learn their goals and find anything else they've touched.",
     "feedback": "Watching is sometimes right, when you need to see a whole operation before tipping the attacker off. Here the attack is already public. Customers see the defacement every minute it stays up, and the attacker can run commands on a server that may reach internal systems. You'd need explicit sign-off from the CFO and legal."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
